imous Safe/Solid Republican ratings from all ten raters", "Climer's dominant fundraising: roughly $884,000 raised and $600,000 on hand versus about $140,000 for Dittmer", "Open seat after Ralph Norman's retirement, but the Republican nominee enters with establishment backing and national fundraising support", "No public general-election polling; a strong Democratic midterm environment is the main scenario for a closer race"], "rationale": "The district's eleven-point Republican lean, unanimous Safe or Solid Republican ratings, and Climer's roughly six-to-one fundraising advantage all point to a comfortable Republican hold of this open seat. A potentially favorable midterm environment for Democrats is the only countervailing evidence, and it leaves a narrow tail of uncertainty rather than genuine competitiveness.", "watch": "Any public polling showing Dittmer within single digits, or signs of an unusually strong Democratic national wave, would make this race worth re-examining.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings.", "Open seat: Ralph Norman (R) is not on the ballot."]}, "overview": "South Carolina's 5th District sits in strongly Republican territory, with a partisan lean of about eleven points toward the GOP, and Ralph Norman's retirement to run for governor leaves the seat open but not demonstrably competitive. State Senator Wes Climer emerged as the clear Republican nominee, raising roughly $884,000 to about $140,000 for Democrat Mallory Dittmer, who claimed her primary victory in June. National figures such as Jim Jordan have headlined Climer's fundraising efforts.\n\nAll ten major race raters call the district Safe or Solid Republican, with The Economist the least emphatic at Likely R, and prediction markets price Climer's win chances around ninety percent. No public general-election polls are available, so uncertainty rests on fundamentals rather than survey data; a strong Democ